<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Lament · c. 1924 · Islamic Caliphates &amp; Middle East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Morq-e Sahar (Bird of the Dawn)</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fa" dir="rtl">مرغ سحر</bdi> / <bdi lang="fa-Latn">Morgh-e Sahar</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Persian song from the 1920s about a caged bird at dawn. Iranians have sung it for a hundred years to ask for freedom.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Mohammad-Taqi Bahar</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Morteza Neidavoud</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Iranians; Iranian movements for constitutional government and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Tehran, Iran</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>early 20th century, after the Iranian Constitutional Revolution; first sung in public 1924; first recorded 1927</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Mohammad-Taqi Bahar; music by Morteza Neydavud)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Morq-e Sahar&quot; is a tasnif, a Persian art song, with words by the poet Mohammad-Taqi Bahar and music by Morteza Neidavoud. It was made in the early 20th century in the wake of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-constitutional-revolution">Iranian Constitutional Revolution</a>, and it has often been sung in protest against injustice, dictatorship and tyranny in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; it is often called the &quot;anthem&quot; of Iran's struggles for freedom.</p><p>The first singer to perform it was Qamar-ol-Moluk Vaziri, who sang it in 1924 at a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> hotel in her first formal concert. It was first recorded in 1927 by Iranoddole Helen (Taj Esfahani); one of the first recordings is also attributed to Moluk Zarrabi. The song has two stanzas. The first was described as the more lyrical and was often sung in public; the second, which deals with social and political questions, was sung at private parties, where talk turned more easily to politics. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah Pahlavi</a> heard the song performed at a gathering in the home of his minister Teymourtash, he banned the second stanza in 1925, and it stayed banned until his forced abdication in 1941.</p><p>Many Iranian singers have performed it since, among them Mohammad-Reza Shajarian, Homayoun Shajarian, Leila Forouhar, Farhad Mehrad, Shakila, Mohsen Namjoo, Reza Sadeghi and Hengameh Akhavan. It became the signature piece of Mohammad-Reza Shajarian, who was widely seen as a public voice for democracy and closed all his concerts with it. When Shajarian died in 2020, crowds of mourners gathered at the Jam Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, where he had been treated, sang &quot;Morq-e Sahar&quot; and chanted slogans against authoritarian rule, which led to a confrontation with the Iranian authorities.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of lament and of longing for freedom, built on the image of the dawn bird of its title. It was heard in its time as a call for liberty.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
